+## v18.33 (warm whites, lane warm1)
+
+The color temperature setting (NEUTRAL, WARM 1..5) now also warms Game Boy (DMG) games. Built with place option 1,
+route option 2 (build w1833b: 0 setup / 0 hold violations; logic 96%, CLS 99%, BSRAM 55 of 56; .bin 774,116 bytes,
+.fs sha256 4600ac260694724034146dcdc395bc391e22fc5b0a3b1a2bff712b7a95a4f0ba). place 2 route 2 (w1833a) left 19 setup
+violations on this tree, so 18.32's own settings do not close it. Like every Shiny Relay image it is a custom build
+(the version's debug bit is set).
+
+### Changed
+- video.v: the warm shift (bright pixels only: red up, green half, blue down, the same levels) was applied only when the
+  core ran in Game Boy Color mode (apply_color_temp = isGBC_mode). It now applies to every pixel. A Game Boy game's
+  colors (the built-in palettes, custom palettes from the ESP32, the boot ROM's colorization) all end in lcd_rgb_raw, so
+  one stage covers every palette. The rule "only bright colors move, dark ones are unchanged" is the old one: a pixel with
+  fewer than two bright channels (bit 4 clear in two or more) is never moved, level 0 (NEUTRAL) adds and subtracts 0.
+  Removing the enable mux costs no logic; the build costs +143 LUT/ALU and no registers (placement noise).
+- system_monitor.sv: unchanged except the version. The level command (0xE) was already taken in every mode; the
+  hardware hotkey keeps its meaning (GB mode: palette, GBC mode: color temperature), the level is set from the menu.
+- Sim: esp32t/sim_cwl/tb_warm.v + run_tb_warm.sh run the stage of this video.v against the 18.32 video.v over all
+  32,768 pixels x levels 0..7 x both modes: a GBC game identical to 18.32, NEUTRAL identical to the raw pixel, darks
+  unmoved, red never falls and blue never rises, monotonic in the level (524,288 cases, PASS).
+
